d report what changed. Do\012  not repeat unchanged discovery or failed checks merely to produce activity.\012  Preserve only the current review and its actionable reason in hot state; older\012  versions are already retained by runner-owned archived snapshots.\012\012Select the acceptance gate:\012\0121. Read the source roadmap, then the maintained Markdown checkbox checklist and\012   compact current handoff in the tracking file. Consult historical entries for\012   evidence, not as a competing task list. If no checklist exists, create it in\012   the tracking file from the original roadmap's acceptance gates without changing\012   their scope or deleting history. An empty tracking scaffold is not completion.\0122. Resume the current handoff active unchecked gate. If none is named, choose the first\012   dependency-ready unchecked gate in roadmap order. Before editing, state its\012   exact checkbox ID/text, acceptance criteria and necessary prerequisites.\012   Obey any stricter per-iteration rule written in the roadmap itself. For example,\012   if the roadmap says each run may process only one checkbox, do not complete,\012   check, or otherwise process a second checkbox in the same invocation.\0123. Compare the current handoff and latest supervisor review with the actual\012   repository state. Do not load prior iteration narratives merely to reconstruct\012   chronology. Resolve the missing active-gate dependency when it is authorized\012   and feasible; size or difficulty alone is not a blocker. If it needs unavailable access, an external service, user\012   judgment or another unmet dependency, leave its checkbox open and record the\012   evidence, unblock condition and next ready checkbox. Continue other useful\012   authorized work in this invocation if coding has not started, or hand it off\012   for the next iteration after finishing the current coherent batch.\0124. Revisit a deferred gate only when its unblock condition changes or a planned\012   boun