peat unchanged discovery or failed checks merely to produce activity.\012  Preserve only the current review and its actionable reason in hot state; older\012  versions are already retained by runner-owned archived snapshots.\012\012Select the acceptance gate:\012\0121. Read the source roadmap, then the maintained Markdown checkbox checklist and\012   compact current handoff in the tracking file. Consult historical entries for\012   evidence, not as a competing task list. If no checklist exists, create it in\012   the tracking file from the original roadmap's acceptance gates without changing\012   their scope or deleting history. An empty tracking scaffold is not completion.\0122. Resume the current handoff active unchecked gate. If none is named, choose the first\012   dependency-ready unchecked gate in roadmap order. Before editing, state its\012   exact checkbox ID/text, acceptance criteria and necessary prerequisites.\012   Obey any stricter per-iteration rule written in the roadmap itself. For example,\012   if the roadmap says each run may process only one checkbox, do not complete,\012   check, or otherwise process a second checkbox in the same invocation.\0123. Compare the current handoff and latest supervisor review with the actual\012   repository state. Do not load prior iteration narratives merely to reconstruct\012   chronology. Resolve the missing active-gate dependency when it is authorized\012   and feasible; size or difficulty alone is not a blocker. If it needs unavailable access, an external service, user\012   judgment or another unmet dependency, leave its checkbox open and record the\012   evidence, unblock condition and next ready checkbox. Continue other useful\012   authorized work in this invocation if coding has not started, or hand it off\012   for the next iteration after finishing the current coherent batch.\0124. Revisit a deferred gate only when its unblock condition changes or a planned\012   bounded retry is due. Keep a compact deferred-gates list in the handoff so\012   fresh contexts do not repeat discovery or silently forget the original gate.\012   Resume it when ready; do not replace its criteria with smaller adjacent tests.\012\0125. Before coding, write a Batch plan in the current handoff: one concrete outcome,\012   original parent gate, related child IDs, shared harness/setup, combined checks\012   and explicit session exit criteria. Size this plan for roughly 30 minutes of\012   implementation. Newly discovered children remain steps toward this outcome;\012   completing one child cannot redefine the selected batch as finished.\0126. For integration work, compose the required canonical harness first, including\012   real middleware, transport and source families required by the selected proof.\012   Reuse it across related cases in the same session. If an essential boundary\012   remains prepared, carry that gap as unfinished batch work and address it before\012   final validation when feasible. A narrower prepared test is prerequisite\012   evidence, not a reason to defer the missing integration boundary indefinitely.\012\012Implement and verify:\012\012- Target roughly 30 minutes of implementation per invocation, excluding initial\012  discovery and final verification. This is a planning target, not a hard deadline\012  or a minimum to fill with unnecessary work. Plan several related ready child\012  tasks under one parent gate that share code, context and verification.\012- For larger roadmaps, the recommended total worker-session budget is **1 hour**:\012  discovery, implementation, feature verification and compact handoff all fit\012  inside it. Use that allowance to deliver a meaningful feature or coherent\012  milestone, not isolated fragments followed by another bootstrap. The approximate\012  30-minute implementation target is a planning aid, not a mandatory exit point.\012  Leave time for verification and handoff before the configured timeout; do not\012  assume an extra hour for those steps or wait for forced termination. This is\012  not a minimum duration and does not change the configured runner timeout.\012  Stricter roadmap limits and the actual configured timeout always take precedence.\012  Do not change those settings or add unrelated work just to fill the allowance.\012- Keep the declared batch outcome stable. Revise it only for an evidenced change\012  in requirements/dependencies or a real blocker, and record the reason. Completing\012  a new nested checkbox or one source-family case is not a session exit criterion.\012- A checkbox is a progress unit, not a session boundary. After finishing a child,\012  continue the next related ready child in the same session until the planned\012  batch is complete or the remaining session budget must be reserved for\012  verification and handoff. Finish the current coherent coding unit within that\012  budget; do not stop just because one small checkbox is done or begin an unrelated\012  major gate to fill time. If the feature remains incomplete, record its exact\012  next implementation step and pending verification rather than claiming completion.\012- If a gate is too large, break it into ordered, independently verifiable child\012  checkboxes with stable IDs under the same parent. Complete a coherent group\012  per run. Size and difficulty require decomposition, not deferral. Stop earlier\012  if the parent gate closes, no related authorized work can advance, or a stricter\012  explicit roadmap run limit applies. Leave unfinished children for the handoff.\012- A local prerequisite or prepared component test does not close an end-to-end\012  gate. Keep the parent unchecked until its full stated criteria pass; record\012  prerequisite progress with child checkboxes under that parent. Child completion\012  is measurable progress, not a replacement for the full parent acceptance gate.\012- Add follow-up checkboxes for discoveries, repairs and missing verification only\012  when necessary to satisfy an original requirement. Each must identify its\012  original parent ID, the exact acceptance criterion it serves, a concrete result\012  and verification. Keep scope, permissions and completion standards unchanged.\012  Record unrelated opportunities separately as out of scope; do not add them to\012  the actionable checklist or treat them as completion dependencies.\012- **Implementation before feature verification.** Do not spend an iteration merely\012  rerunning the active feature's acceptance/integration tests. Implement the\012  planned feature/batch first; run its feature-level verification once the batch\012  is believed complete enough to satisfy the selected criteria.\012- A failed feature check is durable evidence, not an invitation to immediately\012  rerun it. Across iterations, do not rerun the same failed feature check until\012  implementation materially changes code/configuration relevant to that failure.\012  Record the failed check and the required implementation change in Current handoff.\012- After a feature check fails, the next useful action is normally implementation\012  or repair. A verification-only next iteration is allowed only when the roadmap\012  task itself is explicitly verification/diagnosis/reproduction work, or when a\012  concrete external/environmental condition changed and rerunning the check is\012  necessary to distinguish that change. Record that exception explicitly.\012- Finish the selected coding batch before validation; do not run the same checks\012  after each child. Validate the completed batch once, collect related failures,\012  finish the repair batch, and rerun only failed or invalidated checks **after a\012  relevant repair**. Reuse passing evidence for unchanged code and environments.\012- Inspect executed, passed, failed and skipped counts. A skipped check is not a\012  pass. Distinguish prepared tests, actual integration and deployed behavior.\012- Use existing authorization for tests and side e